    "rendered_prompt": "<|im_start|>system\nYou are a helpful assistant.<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>user\nLet $f(x) = 2x - 3$ and $g(f(x)) = 5-4x.$ Find $g(4).$\n\nThink step by step inside <think> </think> tags, then give your final answer inside \\boxed{}.<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\nTo find \\( g(4) \\), we need to determine what value of \\( x \\) makes \\( f(x) = 4 \\), because then \\( g(f(x)) = g(4) \\). Given the function \\( f(x) = 2x - 3 \\), we start by setting \\( f(x) = 4 \\) and solving for \\( x \\).\n\n\\[\nf(x) = 2x - 3 = 4\n\\]\n\nAdd 3 to both sides:\n\n\\[\n2x = 7\n\\]\n\nDivide both sides by 2:\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{7}{2}\n\\]\n\nNow we know that \\( f\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right) = 4 \\). Next, we use the given function \\( g(f(x)) = 5 - 4x \\) to find \\( g(4) \\). Since \\( f\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right) = 4 \\), we substitute \\( x = \\frac{7}{2} \\) into \\( g(f(x)) \\):\n\n\\[\ng(4) = g(f\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)) = 5 - 4\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)\n\\]\n\nSimplify the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n\\[\n5 - 4 \\cdot \\frac{7}{2} = 5 - 2 \\cdot 7 = 5 - 14 = -9\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\( g(4) \\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{-9}\n\\]<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>user\nReview the solution above. Identify and correct any errors; if it is correct, retain its answer. Give your final answer inside \\boxed{}.<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\n",
